-- title:
--   Finite support of orders in a finite function-field extension
-- statement:
--   For a finite extension of the field generated by a transcendental element over C, a fixed function has nonzero order at only finitely many normalized places. One proof route is to choose a finite integral model over C[t] after inverting finitely many base polynomials: both f and f⁻¹ are integral away from finitely many base places, so their orders vanish at every place above the remaining places. Each base place has finitely many extensions in a finite field extension. The statement is consistent at f = 0, whose assigned order is zero everywhere.
-- source:
--   openai/math Approximation FibreNormalRigidity: nonconstant fibre coordinates imply quantitative contact bound.

